    <fr:title text="Formal Specification and Verification">Formal Specification and Verification</fr:title>
    <fr:meta name="toc">true</fr:meta>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    Formal verification is the practice of mathematically proving code to be <html:em>correct</html:em> with regards to a specification (a formal description).
    This can be done in many ways, most interestingly (in my opinion) within <fr:link href="/dhsorens-000B/" title="Interactive Theorem Provers" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-000B/" display-uri="dhsorens-000B" type="local">interactive theorem provers</fr:link> like <fr:link href="/dhsorens-lean/" title="The Lean Theorem Prover" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-lean/" display-uri="dhsorens-lean" type="local">Lean</fr:link> or <fr:link href="/dhsorens-rocq/" title="The Rocq Theorem Prover" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-rocq/" display-uri="dhsorens-rocq" type="local">Rocq</fr:link>.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
